{# The members-area library grid, grouped by category.

   Renders entirely from site/_data/library.json off the page's own `lang`,
   using the `memberUrl` / `memberCta` / `memberDesc` fields — NOT the `desc`
   and `url` fields, which are the sales-page wording and deliberately differ.

   Added Sep 4, 2026, replacing the hand-written card grids in
   site/members/index.njk, site/members/en/index.njk and
   site/members/pt/index.njk. Those three files each listed all 13 entries by
   hand and were the open-loops item "three more files that will drift the next
   time a tool ships or retires" — which is exactly what had already happened to
   the sales pages. Shipping a tool is now one edit to library.json.

   The ES home previously carried six filter chips over a flat grid; three of
   the six matched exactly one card and "Fuerza" held six unrelated ones
   (two core routines, three injury protocols and a strength guide). EN and PT
   had no filters at all. Grouped headings replace them: a reader scanning three
   labelled blocks does not need to click anything, and a category that holds
   one card is a tag, not a category.

   NOTE: filtering is an inline `{% if item.category == cat %}`, not selectattr —
   Nunjucks is not Jinja2 and its selectattr/equalto silently returned the whole
   list here, rendering all 13 cards under all three headings. `categoryCounts`
   in library.json answers "is this bucket empty" without a filter. #}
{%- set lib = library[lang] or library.es -%}
{%- set order = lib.categoryOrder or ["train", "pain", "numbers"] -%}
{%- for cat in order %}
  {%- if lib.categoryCounts[cat] %}
  <div class="lib-group">
    <h3 class="lib-group-title">{{ lib.categories[cat] }}</h3>
    <div class="grid-3">
      {%- for item in lib.live %}{% if item.category == cat %}
      <div class="card">
        <span class="tag">{{ item.tag }}</span>
        <h3>{{ item.name }}</h3>
        <p>{{ item.memberDesc or item.desc }}</p>
        <a class="card-link" href="{{ item.memberUrl }}">{{ item.memberCta }}</a>
      </div>
      {%- endif %}{% endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
  {%- endif %}
{%- endfor %}
